Imports:
  - Types:
      - Backend
    From: server/storage/backend

Usages:
  callback_decoupling: |
    This cell's own comments state the reason for its two function-type extension points
    (RangeDeleter, Checkpointer): "defined here to avoid circular dependency with mvcc". The
    mvcc cell and the etcdserver cell each push a closure into this cell (SetRangeDeleter,
    SetCheckpointer) rather than this cell importing them — keep new cross-cutting lease
    behavior on this same pattern instead of adding a new outbound import.

Annotations: |
  This cell owns TTL-based key expiration grouped under lease IDs: granting/revoking/renewing/
  checkpointing leases, tracking which keys are attached to which lease, and running a
  background loop that finds expired leases and due checkpoints on a fixed tick. It persists
  lease metadata directly against the imported `Backend`. It decouples itself from the two
  callers that must react to lease events (the mvcc cell, which deletes the actual keys on
  expiry/revocation, and the etcdserver cell, which turns a checkpoint into a proposed raft
  request) via `callback_decoupling`, rather than importing either of them.

  Use `Backend` for lease persistence exactly as the imported contract describes.

---

"Lessor(be Backend, cfg LessorConfig)":
  location: lessor.go
  annotations: |
    Owns every lease: grants, revocations, renewals, checkpointing, and the attach/detach
    relationship between leases and the keys backed by them.

    `be`: the imported `Backend` used to persist lease records.
    `cfg`: minimum TTL, checkpoint interval, and checkpoint-persistence settings.
  methods:
    "Grant(id int64, ttlSeconds int64) -> lease:Lease, err:error": |
      Create a new lease that expires at least `ttlSeconds` from now.
    "Revoke(id int64) -> err:error": |
      Revoke a lease immediately, invoking the registered range-deleter callback (see
      `callback_decoupling`) to delete every key attached to it.
    "Renew(id int64) -> ttlSeconds:int64, err:error": |
      Reset a lease's expiry to a full TTL from now.
    "Lookup(id int64) -> lease:Lease": |
      Return the lease for `id`, or nil if it does not exist or has expired.
    "Attach(id int64, items []LeaseItem) -> err:error": |
      Associate `items` (keys) with an existing lease.
    "Detach(id int64, items []LeaseItem) -> err:error": |
      Remove the association between `items` and a lease, without revoking the lease itself.
    "Promote(extend Duration)": |
      Become the primary lessor responsible for expiring and renewing leases, extending every
      existing lease's TTL by `extend` to smooth over the leadership transition.
    "Demote()": |
      Stop acting as the primary lessor (e.g. on loss of raft leadership).
    "Checkpoint(id int64, remainingTTL int64) -> err:error": |
      Apply a previously-agreed remaining TTL to a lease, used after the registered
      checkpointer callback (see `callback_decoupling`) round-trips a checkpoint through raft
      consensus.
    "ExpiredLeasesC() -> leases:[]Lease": |
      Delivered as a channel of expired-lease batches in the real implementation; consumed by
      the caller to drive revocation.
    "SetRangeDeleter(rangeDeleter RangeDeleterFunc)": |
      Register the callback used to delete keys on lease revocation/expiry. See
      `callback_decoupling`.
    "SetCheckpointer(checkpointer CheckpointerFunc)": |
      Register the callback used to propose a checkpoint through raft consensus. See
      `callback_decoupling`.

"Lease()":
  location: lease.go
  annotations: |
    One granted lease: its ID, remaining TTL, and the set of keys currently attached to it.
  properties:
    "ID -> int64": |
      Unique lease identifier.
  methods:
    "TTL() -> ttlSeconds:int64": |
      The full TTL this lease was granted or last renewed with.
    "Remaining() -> remaining:Duration": |
      Time remaining until this lease expires.
    "Keys() -> keys:[]string": |
      Every key currently attached to this lease.

---

Author: Goga
CreatedAt: 26/08/26
Description: |
  TTL-based key-expiration subsystem. Depends only on `server/storage/backend` for persistence;
  decouples from the mvcc/etcdserver cells that react to its events via injected callbacks
  rather than an outbound import.
